 1.1 The space $H_0^1(\Omega)$ is a closed linear subspace of the complete space $H^1(\Omega)$ by [F1] and [F3]. Since $\Omega$ is bounded, it lies in a slab $a<x\cdot e<b$ for some unit vector $e$ and finite $a<b$; applying [F2] with $p=2$ gives $\|v\|_{L^2}\le C(2)(b-a)\|\nabla v\|_{L^2}$ for every $v\in H_0^1(\Omega)$. Thus $\|v\|_H:=\|\nabla v\|_{L^2}$ is a norm equivalent to the inherited $H^1$ norm, and $H_0^1(\Omega)$ is a real Hilbert space for $(v,w)_H=\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla w\,dx$. [F1, F2, F3, given]
 
-1.2 Fix any representative $U\in H^1(\Omega)$ of $f$; the existence argument below is independent of which representative is fixed. On $H_0^1(\Omega)$ define $a_\gamma(z,v)=\int_\Omega\gamma\nabla z\cdot\nabla v\,dx$ and $F_U(v)=-\int_\Omega\gamma\nabla U\cdot\nabla v\,dx$. By [F6], $a_\gamma$ is bounded by $C\|z\|_H\|v\|_H$, coercive by $c\|z\|_H^2$, and $F_U$ is bounded by $C\|\nabla U\|_{L^2}\|v\|_H$. Countable Choice follows from [F5], so [F4] gives a unique $z\in H_0^1(\Omega)$ with $a_\gamma(z,v)=F_U(v)$ for every $v\in H_0^1(\Omega)$. Then $u_{\gamma,f}:=U+z$ has quotient class $f$ and satisfies the stated weak equation. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1]
