-2.2 The weak solution minimizes the quadratic energy in its boundary class: if $W$ is any representative of $f$, then $W-u_{\gamma,f}\in H_0^1(\Omega)$, and expanding the square with the weak equation gives $\int_\Omega\gamma|\nabla W|^2\,dx=\int_\Omega\gamma|\nabla u_{\gamma,f}|^2\,dx+\int_\Omega\gamma|\nabla(W-u_{\gamma,f})|^2\,dx$. Using [F6] and taking the infimum over representatives yields $\|\nabla u_{\gamma,f}\|_{L^2}\le\sqrt{C/c}\,\|f\|_{\mathcal B_\Omega}$. The same infimum argument gives representatives $V$ of $h$ with $\|V\|_{H^1}\le\|h\|_{\mathcal B_\Omega}+\varepsilon$ for every $\varepsilon>0$. Cauchy–Schwarz and the upper bound in [F6] now give $|\langle\Lambda_\gamma f,h\rangle|\le C\sqrt{C/c}\,\|f\|_{\mathcal B_\Omega}(\|h\|_{\mathcal B_\Omega}+\varepsilon)$; letting $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ proves boundedness. [F1, F6, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] To prove bilinearity, for $f_1,f_2\in\mathcal B_\Omega$ and $a,b\in\mathbb R$, the function $a u_{\gamma,f_1}+b u_{\gamma,f_2}$ has boundary class $af_1+bf_2$ and satisfies the weak equation by linearity of the quotient map and of the integral. Uniqueness from step 1.3 therefore gives $u_{\gamma,af_1+bf_2}=a u_{\gamma,f_1}+b u_{\gamma,f_2}$. For $h_1,h_2\in\mathcal B_\Omega$, representatives $V_{h_1},V_{h_2}$ give $aV_{h_1}+bV_{h_2}$ as a representative of $ah_1+bh_2$; representative independence from step 2.1 and linearity of the integral show that the energy pairing is linear in its second argument as well. Thus the energy form is bilinear, and the bound just proved makes it a bounded bilinear form on $\mathcal B_\Omega\times\mathcal B_\Omega$. [F1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
